hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well we don't have a  full fledged theory of justice you know  totally spelled out with Epicurus as we  do with many other philosophers in part  because we only possess a few of his  works in the present out of the many  works that he wrote but we do have some  important discussions of justice  primarily occurring in his principle  doctrines towards the end of them but  with a few other remarks scattered here  and there in the corpus and he's going  to deal with essentially two main topics  the nature of justice what is justice  and then because of the way he  understands justice we also have to  think about the usefulness of justice  and why is justice useful how does  usefulness fit in with us and if we're  thinking about this in terms of Epicurus  as being a hedonist philosopher as being  you know one of the art main articulate  errs but hedonist theory then you might  say well why isn't he talking directly  about pains and pleasures we're gonna  see that he will talk a little bit about  pain but why isn't he focused on justice  as a maximizing pleasure and minimizing  pain or something like that why talk  about usefulness well you know one  reason is because unlike many of the  other  Virtue's or states of character justice  actually doesn't focus in the ancient  world of these primarily on pain and  pleasure it is understood as something  that has a value in its own right or  that has some sort of usefulness on the  sort of profitable nough some sort of  beneficial status for other things like  you know you might say well it's good  for society or it's good for getting  things done and Epicurus does indeed  have a view on justice very much like  that so let's start with in a question  what is justice now he tells us this is  a very radical position there never was  and by extension there never is or will  be anything like absolute justice  justice per se justice in a simple way  that applies across the board to  everybody instead what we find he says  is natural justice that is justice that  is arising within human communities  within human beings that are that are  natural creatures it's an expression of  usefulness he says it's an expression or  a symbol of usefulness  now usefulness for what well primarily  for preventing people from harming other  people or from being harmed so there's  already a kind of reciprocity in there  you can talk about justice within say a  friendship or within a family or within  a military troop you can also talk about  it as within a whole society as well and  Epicurus has all these sorts of things  in mind so this is the primary purpose  of justice justice understood in a sort  of broad sense it is something that is  useful for keeping the peace keeping us  from harming each other keeping us from  being harmed by other people there's a  kind of trade-off there so he says he  goes so far as to say that justice  really depends on a kind of agreement so  many people have seen in Epicurus a  early formulation of what's called  social calm  tract theory we're either in an explicit  way or in some sort of implicit way  people get together within a society and  they say look how can we actually live  together how can we keep ourselves from  being harmed from each other given that  you know we have tendencies to do things  that we know are right to steal to lie  to cheat to injure I'll make an  agreement with you and you make an  agreement with me not to do these sorts  of things and then we'll have to have  some sort of authority who can actually  enforce this rule or perhaps will  enforce the rule there's a lot of  different variations on this and that  the curious doesn't worry too much about  which particular variation we're going  to have but you just say it stems from a  mutual agreement with fellow human  beings in society not to harm each other  not to do things that are going to  create problems for each other he also  goes so far as to say that it varies by  application in laws of specific  communities so what is just in one  community it is enshrined in the laws  there may not in fact be just in another  community or even in the laws there  there could be laws that if you go from  one community to another community seem  to contradict each other and for  epicures it's not a problem because  there isn't any single standard of  justice across the board that gives you  laws you know in a strict sense that  says this must be the case in all cases  you won't find some regularities if you  don't have some way of regulating  violence who is allowed to use violence  in what situations who's allowed to use  deadly force well then you're going to  have you know a lot of people harming  each other there is almost always some  rules about sexuality who is allowed to  do what with whom there's almost always  laws about property but the exact laws  the specifics of them are going to vary  from community to community depending on  what they actually find useful and what  may be useful in one time in one culture  in one set of circumstances may not  actually be useful later on so Epicurus  is quite alright with  the notion of revising laws it's not as  if once you make a contract with  everybody if there isn't even a contract  you just as agreement that you stuck  with it once you know once and for all  time you know you you can't change it at  all Epicurus says no is over here with  the usefulness part when the laws cease  to be useful they don't express justice  anymore  that is the purpose of the laws to  express them to articulate and to give  us some guidance about what is actually  just but it's going to be particularized  to the community to which one belongs so  if you're moving from one community to  another if you're moving say from  Corinth to Rome you know that old  expression When in Rome do as the Romans  do Epicurus would say exactly right if  the Romans have some laws and there are  good laws on the books about something  that the Corinthians you know the Romans  forbid something that the Corinthians  allow well if you're in Rome then to be  just means to follow those those rules  not because simply law is is you know  good in and of itself but because law  enshrines this mutual agreement that  keeps us from harming each other so that  leads to this question of usefulness why  is justice useful why is it something  that is beneficial to us yeah there's  three main things that Epicurus talk so  I'm actually going to start with this  bottom one here since we began with  thinking about Society so there's four  I'm sorry he says when the laws are no  longer useful  the laws are no longer just they no  longer express justice this shouldn't be  a surprise to us he thinks if we get  upset about this sort of thing we say  look the law back in 1789 was against  this and look at people are doing that  now we can we can say well is it  actually harming anyone for them to do  that activity now maybe the people back  then were you know somehow harmed by it  but we're not anymore so  we can take that law off the books now  why is justice useful in a personal  sense here we start getting to questions  of pleasure and pain and the freedom  from pain that constitutes a certain  kind of pleasure so  Epicurus says justice provides pleasure  and it prevents pain it really doesn't  provide much pleasure in and of itself  you might think of it as sort of  facilitating the conditions under which  pleasure can occur but it certainly does  prevent pain now it prevents pain in in  multiple ways right  in one way it keeps other people from  harming us so we can live securely with  other people and it keeps us from doing  that too if we abide by the rules so  that's important if we want to enjoy a  good life we need some sort of norms  governing them that we can live  according to that aren't you know to  oppress in it or to owner us but which  we can say yeah that makes sense we  should go along with that I know that my  feelings might make me go this way but  the law says this so I'm gonna follow  the law we count on other people doing  the same thing so justice keeps us from  being harmed right  Epicurus also says and this might be a  bit more debatable if we think about say  sociopaths right the person who violates  the norms of justice the agreement  always has to feel fear or worry about  being detected and he's not just  concerned about justice in terms of the  legal system he's also a like in the  Vatican sayings he says look don't do  anything that you would be ashamed of  your neighbors finding out if you know I  came to light that's also the same sort  of thing if you're doing the wrong thing  and you know that it's the wrong thing  and maybe you say well it's to my  advantage to do it or you know I'm  come by a desire I'm kind of a greedy  guy so I so I take stuff that I'm not  supposed to take or you know when my  neighbors lend me stuff I don't return  it to them or I return it to them in a  bad condition well if you do that  Epicurus thinks that you're going to  have a bad conscience and that by itself  from a hedonist perspective is a bad  thing because having a band or a guilty  conscience means you're worried about  people finding out and you're also  worried if it's like legal justice about  punishment or I suppose if it's personal  interactions about some sort of reprisal  or or shunning or something like that so  there's there's you know two sides to  this there's the don't commit injustice  because then you're gonna be on the outs  with everybody else and that will be  painful for you  and do also commit justice do you follow  the norms of justice precisely because  you're hoping other people will do that  and when we all do that we have a  relatively pain-free existence so that's  where the usefulness of justice really  lies her for Epicurus and that's what  grounds the whole conception of justice  so when he's saying a person cannot live  pleasantly without living prudently well  and justly this is what he means by  living justly and this is why he is  saying that
 

